# Publish image metadata

Publish after the producer successfully builds and pushes the image. The
catalogue service must already exist, and an administrator must grant your
trusted workload repository `publish` access to it.

## Prepare the metadata

Supply the actual pushed tags, OCI image or index digest, and platforms. The
action creates a JSON payload like this synthetic example; replace every value
with metadata from your successful push:

```json
{
  "tags": ["1.2.3", "latest"],
  "digest": "sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa",
  "platforms": [
    {"os": "linux", "architecture": "amd64"},
    {"os": "linux", "architecture": "arm64", "variant": "v8"}
  ]
}
```

List only tags that actually exist upstream. A Git commit SHA is not an image
digest. Publication adds new tags or updates existing mutable tags atomically.
Add `"immutable": true` only when the supplied upstream tags must permanently
keep the same image and platforms. That policy is private and cannot later be
removed from the tag.

Requests allow at most 32 tags, 32 platforms and 1 MiB of JSON. Unknown fields,
notes, source URLs, annotations and raw build webhooks are rejected. An optional
`reference` can carry a tag or digest, but explicit fields must agree; its
registry and repository portion is discarded before persistence.

## Connect a Forgejo job

Use the <%= link_to 'reusable composite action', repository_file('action.yml') %>
after your successful push. It builds the JSON payload and uses the
<%= link_to 'standard-library Ruby publisher', repository_file('examples/publish.rb') %>
to request workload identity and submit the metadata. Adapt the
<%= link_to 'Forgejo job fragment', repository_file('examples/forgejo-job.yaml') %>:

```yaml
jobs:
  build-and-publish:
    runs-on: docker
    enable-openid-connect: true
    steps:
      # Existing checkout, image build and successful push go here.
      - name: Publish image metadata
        # Pin to a reviewed commit containing action.yml before use.
        uses: https://github.com/damacus/release-mirror@main
        with:
          service: widget
          tags: |
            1.2.3
            latest
          digest: sha256:aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa
          platforms: '[{"os":"linux","architecture":"arm64","variant":"v8"}]'
```

The runner must have Ruby, CA certificates and workload identity support; no
Bundler or application gems are needed. Tags are newline-delimited; blank lines
are ignored and tag spelling is preserved. `platforms` is a JSON array of OCI
platform objects, so optional `variant`, `os.version` and `os.features` survive.
Supply platform information from the built image or index rather than inferring
it from the runner. The action creates no tags and does not inspect or push images.
`immutable` defaults to `false`. Set `immutable: 'true'` only for actual immutable
upstream tags. Publish immutable and mutable tags in separate action calls when
they refer to the same digest but require different policies.

The `url` and `audience` inputs both default to `https://releases.damacus.io`.
Override them when using another catalogue; the audience must match the
operator's trusted workload configuration. There are no action outputs; a
rejected publication fails the step. The helper requests a short-lived
identity token and sends the payload to
`POST /api/v1/services/widget/releases`; it does not print tokens or payloads.
Verify the actual Forgejo issuer, repository claim and runner versions before
using a live producer. Forgejo disables workload identity for pull requests from
forks. Use a full remote action URL and pin a reviewed commit in production.

For an existing producer that already writes a JSON payload, the standalone
publisher still accepts `CATALOGUE_PAYLOAD_FILE` alongside `CATALOGUE_URL`,
`CATALOGUE_SERVICE` and `CATALOGUE_AUDIENCE`.

## Diagnose a rejected request

| Response | Check |
| --- | --- |
| `401` | Token issuer, audience, expiry, signing keys and expected token type |
| `403` | Exact repository ID has `publish` for this service |
| `404` | Administrator has created the service alias |
| `409` | Existing tag's immutable policy conflicts with the update |
| `413` | Request is within the 1 MiB limit |
| `415` | Request has `Content-Type: application/json` |
| `422` | Tags, digest, platforms and allowed fields match the schema |
| `503` | Configured identity-provider endpoint is available |

Errors deliberately omit rejected values. Inspect configuration and synthetic
requests without printing credentials or raw identity tokens. Check the
<%= link_to 'API reference', '/api.html' %> for the full contract.
